  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Tricholoma subsejunctum is a medium-sized woodland mushroom characterized by its grayish-brown cap and distinctive gill arrangement. This species belongs to the Tricholomataceae family and is found in forest ecosystems throughout New England during fall months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Gill Attachment",
      "description": "Gills are notched near the stem (subsejunct), distinguishing it from closely related species with fully attached or free gills",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Cap Color and Texture",
      "description": "Gray-brown to olive-brown cap with a slightly fibrous or streaked appearance, often with a subtle umbo or flat center",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Characteristics",
      "description": "Pale, cylindrical stem that may show subtle longitudinal fibrils and lacks a distinct ring or bulbous base",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Tricholoma subsejunctum grows on forest floors in mixed hardwood and coniferous forests throughout New England, typically fruiting in autumn.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The scientific name 'subsejunctum' refers to the gill attachment pattern, where 'sub-' means nearly or somewhat, and 'sejunctum' refers to separated or notched gills, making this a key feature for identification.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
